Rules:
  [D1] Use specific image versions, not `latest`.
  [D2] Use official base images (alpine, debian, distroless).
  [D3] Use `.dockerignore` to reduce build context.
  [D4] Combine `RUN` commands to reduce layers.
  [D5] Use `WORKDIR` to set working directory.
  [D6] Use `COPY` instead of `ADD` for local files.
  [D7] Use `EXPOSE` to document ports.
  [D8] Use `CMD` or `ENTRYPOINT` for container startup.

ReferenceImplementation:
  ProjectRule_R91:
    rule: compose services never reference external/registry images — every
      service uses `build:` with the project's own Dockerfile (see repo
      root Dockerfile + docker-compose.yml: sdd-sandbox builds locally,
      pinned digest, non-root, healthcheck).
    why: owning your own image means tomorrow's problem is found in YOUR
      image, not a global one you cannot debug.
  ProjectRule_R92:
    rule: all bind mounts live under project-root var/
      (./var/sandbox:/var/sandbox) — named volumes forbidden.
  ProjectRule_R102:
    rule: resource limits mandatory even when upstream compose defines
      none (cpus, memory, pids_limit, no-new-privileges, read_only rootfs).

Pros:
  - Consistent environments
  - Isolation
  - Portability

Cons:
  - Learning curve
  - Image size
  - Security complexity

Alternatives:
  - Podman
  - Containerd
  - Buildpacks

State: +

